{
  "wasim_version": "0.1.0",
  "source": {
    "generator": null,
    "notes": "Monte Carlo GREEKS of a cash-or-nothing digital call, comparing the likelihood-ratio method (LRM) with bump-and-revalue. The price is a discontinuous payoff; its delta is where naive MC struggles. LRM (delta = E[payoff * score], score = Z/(S0 sigma sqrt(T))) leaves the payoff untouched so the discontinuity is irrelevant and the variance is low and eps-free; central bump with common random numbers has the same mean but far higher variance (a rare nonzero when K falls between the two bumped terminals). Both match the analytic delta disc*n(d2)/(S0 sigma sqrt(T)). NEEDS NO NEW ENGINE FEATURE: the driving normal Z is an explicit random_variable, so the LRM score is a plain expression. See DIGITAL_OPTION_SCOPE.md."
  },
